[TITLE]
E3 - Vasconcelos et al. (2006) negative piezometric pressure (siphon) experiment. Issue #156.
Same pipeline as E2 with the center raised 0.15 m: first half +2.0% (upstream to center),
second half -2.0%. Both ends at elev 0. System filled to 0.30 m at the fill box, at rest
(fully pressurized: center crown 0.244 < 0.30). Sudden siphon withdrawal 0.45 L/s at the
fill box from t=0. Negative gauge pressure develops at the center as the fill-box level
drops. Comparison valid to t~40 s (air-cavity intrusion afterwards - two-phase, out of scope);
END_TIME is set AT that boundary: past it the TPA column separates and the regime chatter
of a physics the model does not contain (two-phase air transport) ends the run (P5).
Measured: velocity + pressure at x=9.9 m, pressure at x=14.1 m. Paper: a=300 m/s (measured).
STATIONS ARE SEALED JUNCTIONS (SUR_DEPTH 30, P5 revision): a plain junction would be an
atmosphere contact and would (correctly, but wrongly for this deck) vent a TPA latch, but a
SEALED junction (SUR_DEPTH > 0) holds it -- same classification as a virtual junction in
both routers. Virtual junctions cannot carry an initial depth, and under FV that left the
two VJ-to-VJ conduits (C2, C3) seeded EMPTY on a deck whose whole premise is a primed
column: the slot columns spent the first seconds refilling garbage and the TPA columns
diverged outright (P5 measurement). InitDepth on each sealed junction seeds every conduit
full, which is the state the paper starts from.
EXPECTED PHYSICAL CONTRAST: slot-closure columns (C1/C2/C7) revert to free surface when head
drops below crown; TPA columns hold full-pipe sub-atmospheric flow (plan gate A-G4 analog).

